WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.785 --> 00:00:06.410
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Hey there, everybody,

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:08.540
and welcome to CST 103.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:11.360
I'm Mark Mirchandani, developer
advocate at Google Cloud.

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:12.860
And today, we're
going to be talking

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:16.102
about what's new in Google
Cloud cost management.

00:00:16.102 --> 00:00:18.560
We'll go over a brief tour of
the different cost management

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:20.270
tools that are available to you.

00:00:20.270 --> 00:00:23.250
And we'll look at what's
changed over the past year.

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:24.890
Now, we've got a
lot to cover, so I'm

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:26.910
going to go ahead
and get started.

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:27.800
First things first.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:30.110
This URL is where you
can view the billing

00:00:30.110 --> 00:00:32.565
notes for all the different
releases that have happened.

00:00:32.565 --> 00:00:35.190
We're going to go over the ones
that happened in the last year.

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:37.100
But if you need a text
version or if you just

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:38.930
want to see the changes,
make sure to keep

00:00:38.930 --> 00:00:41.090
an eye on that you URL.

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:42.920
So when you talk
about cost management,

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:45.610
we're really talking about
the three key pillars--

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:48.830
understand, control,
and optimize.

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:50.680
So understanding
your cost is all

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:53.740
about being able to see how
your costs are being generated

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:55.870
and where they're trending.

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:58.090
Controlling your cost
is really about reducing

00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:00.400
the risk of
overspending and being

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:03.980
able to improve the
predictability of your costs.

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:07.280
Optimizing is really more
about focusing on overspending.

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:09.530
Again, control is
about controlling it.

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:12.470
Optimizing is really about
eliminating it and then

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:14.690
reducing waste through
some of the recommendations

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:17.120
that Google Cloud
provides and finding ways

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:19.820
that you can get the same
performance for lower cost.

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:21.570
We're going to look
at all three of these.

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:22.945
But before we can
dive into that,

00:01:22.945 --> 00:01:26.708
we really need to look at how
to organize your resources.

00:01:26.708 --> 00:01:29.000
And I don't want to spend
too much time on the resource

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:29.720
hierarchy.

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:31.850
But I highly recommend
you go and check out

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:34.730
last year's next talk on
the resource organization

00:01:34.730 --> 00:01:36.110
and access management.

00:01:36.110 --> 00:01:38.930
It's a really great talk, and
it dives into a lot of detail.

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:41.600
But the overall summary is
that you want your resource

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:44.390
hierarchy to look a lot
like your organization.

00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:49.160
You can see an example here.

00:01:49.160 --> 00:01:50.900
But as you're
trying to dive down

00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:53.960
into who has access
to what projects,

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:56.030
you can use folders
to break that out.

00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:57.800
The permissions are
inherited downwards.

00:01:57.800 --> 00:01:59.840
You also want to be
able to track your costs

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:01.410
through this same hierarchy.

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:03.140
So you want to
understand which groups

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:05.900
are paying for which
resources or which projects

00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:07.730
fall under which categories.

00:02:07.730 --> 00:02:09.889
It's really important to
be able to track that,

00:02:09.889 --> 00:02:12.320
because as your
organization grows,

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:14.390
you're going to want to
be able to say, how do I

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:15.860
know who paid for what?

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:18.800
Another really great feature
you can use is project label.

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:21.430
So you can create another way
of looking at your projects.

00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:23.930
And you might label them with,
first say, your environments,

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:24.280
right?

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:26.000
So you might have a production
environment and a development

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:26.930
environment.

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:28.500
And then you can
say the same thing.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:30.890
How can I get my costs for
my production environment?

00:02:30.890 --> 00:02:33.240
Well, you can grab all the
costs for those labels.

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:35.450
So it's really important
to have those organized.

00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:36.948
And speaking of
permissions, you're

00:02:36.948 --> 00:02:38.990
going to see a lot of
permissions for the billing

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:39.785
account themselves.

00:02:39.785 --> 00:02:42.410
I want to go over the roles that
you're typically going to see.

00:02:42.410 --> 00:02:44.618
But it's also important to
know that you can actually

00:02:44.618 --> 00:02:46.820
create custom roles with
the different permissions

00:02:46.820 --> 00:02:47.565
that you need.

00:02:47.565 --> 00:02:49.940
And you want to always follow
that security best practice

00:02:49.940 --> 00:02:51.570
of least privilege.

00:02:51.570 --> 00:02:53.540
So making sure that people
only have the access

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:55.185
they need and nothing more.

00:02:55.185 --> 00:02:56.810
So you can create
custom roles for that

00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:58.080
as you go down the road.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:00.372
But to start with, you're
going to have billing account

00:03:00.372 --> 00:03:02.960
administrators,
viewers, and users.

00:03:02.960 --> 00:03:05.420
Viewers are only going to
be able to see the cost

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:06.440
on the billing account.

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:07.940
So it's a great
simple role for them

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:10.670
to go ahead and see how
much people are spending.

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:13.700
Users are actually able
to associate a project

00:03:13.700 --> 00:03:14.782
with a billing account.

00:03:14.782 --> 00:03:16.490
And that's really
important that you only

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:19.123
give that to trusted
users because, as, again,

00:03:19.123 --> 00:03:20.540
as your organization
grows, you're

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:22.190
going to have a lot of projects.

00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:24.295
And being able to add them
to the billing account

00:03:24.295 --> 00:03:26.670
means that you're actually
going to have to pay for them.

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:29.150
So make sure that you have
the right permissions set up

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:31.370
and that only certain
users can add projects

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:32.365
to the billing account.

00:03:32.365 --> 00:03:34.490
Finally, you're going to
have the administrator who

00:03:34.490 --> 00:03:36.198
is going to be able
to control everything

00:03:36.198 --> 00:03:37.740
like an administrator would.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:39.740
And it actually is going
to be the focus of most

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:41.990
of the things we're talking
about today because they're

00:03:41.990 --> 00:03:44.073
the ones who can look at
all the different reports

00:03:44.073 --> 00:03:46.100
in the console, as well
as being the ones you

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:47.820
can contact support.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:49.662
So you're going
to probably start

00:03:49.662 --> 00:03:50.870
with using those three roles.

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:52.593
You might add more
roles as you go on.

00:03:52.593 --> 00:03:54.260
The other thing that's
important to note

00:03:54.260 --> 00:03:57.140
is that a project
owner can see the costs

00:03:57.140 --> 00:03:59.120
for only their projects, right?

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:01.970
So they'll have access to the
billing section of a console.

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:04.070
And they'll be able to
see the billing reports.

00:04:04.070 --> 00:04:07.190
But it'll only show data
for their specific projects.

00:04:07.190 --> 00:04:08.970
You can use that
to your advantage

00:04:08.970 --> 00:04:10.970
when you're setting up
the permissions for users

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:13.970
to be able to understand
who can see what.

00:04:13.970 --> 00:04:17.149
So with your costs being
able to be measured only

00:04:17.149 --> 00:04:18.649
because your resources
are organized

00:04:18.649 --> 00:04:20.540
and because you have
these permissions,

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:22.880
let's move on to the first
step, which is really

00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:25.310
understanding your costs.

00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:27.135
And the best way to
understand your cost

00:04:27.135 --> 00:04:29.510
is going to be to go to the
billing section of the Google

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:30.572
Cloud console.

00:04:30.572 --> 00:04:32.030
And as soon as you
do, you're going

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:34.580
to see this which is the
billing account overview.

00:04:34.580 --> 00:04:37.440
This page looks a lot different
than it did a year ago.

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:39.110
And there's been a
ton of changes to it.

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:40.777
We're going to step
through some of now.

00:04:40.777 --> 00:04:43.460
But needless to say, it's
an overview page, right?

00:04:43.460 --> 00:04:45.800
So you can see a lot of
really quick information

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:48.408
about your billing account
as soon as you go there.

00:04:48.408 --> 00:04:50.450
The first section we'll
look at is this new chart

00:04:50.450 --> 00:04:52.283
that actually is two
new charts that show up

00:04:52.283 --> 00:04:55.190
that show you your top five
projects and your top five

00:04:55.190 --> 00:04:57.650
products that you're
spending costs on.

00:04:57.650 --> 00:05:00.595
And it shows you their actual
cost and their forecasted cost.

00:05:00.595 --> 00:05:02.720
So, I mean, as far as going
to your billing account

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:04.280
and seeing important
information,

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:06.632
you're going to be able to
see where your costs are,

00:05:06.632 --> 00:05:08.090
where they're going
over the month.

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:11.090
Just being able to see that
quick view is really helpful.

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:14.357
In addition to that, we've also
added a billing health card.

00:05:14.357 --> 00:05:16.190
So this is a card that
uses some of the best

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:19.400
practices for our customers
and says, hey, maybe you

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:21.500
want to apply these to
your billing account

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:24.470
so that you're able to stay on
top of, well, the health of it.

00:05:24.470 --> 00:05:26.000
So one of the great
examples of this

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:28.250
is being able to set up a
budget, which we'll actually

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:30.320
talk about in a little
bit or making sure

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:33.990
that you turn on BigQuery
export or billing data export

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:34.930
to BigQuery.

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:36.683
We'll also talk
about that later.

00:05:36.683 --> 00:05:38.600
And then one of the other
good recommendations

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:41.640
is to have multiple billing
account administrators.

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:44.180
This can be really important
because, as I mentioned before,

00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:46.138
billing account administrators
are the ones who

00:05:46.138 --> 00:05:47.150
can contact support.

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:48.650
So it's really
critical to make sure

00:05:48.650 --> 00:05:51.740
that you have hopefully at
least two, but maybe more.

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:54.840
And you have this idea of a
reasonable level of redundancy,

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:55.340
right?

00:05:55.340 --> 00:05:57.380
You don't want too many
people having access

00:05:57.380 --> 00:05:58.430
to your billing account.

00:05:58.430 --> 00:06:00.680
But you also want to make
sure that if one person gets

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:04.543
locked out, you're
not completely stuck.

00:06:04.543 --> 00:06:06.960
So one of the first reports
that I want to talk about here

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:08.460
is a new one that's
been launched.

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:10.470
And it's the cost
breakdown report.

00:06:10.470 --> 00:06:14.280
And this really just gives you a
quick and easy-to-see breakdown

00:06:14.280 --> 00:06:16.830
of the net impact of
all the things you're

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:19.980
spending, how the different
discounts or other spendings

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:22.560
affected those, and
then your final cost.

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:24.780
This is a great and
very simple chart

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:26.970
to be able to look at
any month and understand,

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:27.990
here's what I spent.

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:29.520
Here, all the different
Google Cloud discounts.

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:31.840
And there's a lot of different
ones that can happen.

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:35.620
And then here's what I'm going
to end up actually paying.

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:37.920
Besides that, there's
the billing cost table.

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:40.600
Also, all of this is going to
be available on the console.

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:43.360
And one of the reasons that all
of these pages have been added

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:46.210
is because a lot of this data
has actually been removed

00:06:46.210 --> 00:06:48.460
from the Google Cloud invoices.

00:06:48.460 --> 00:06:50.800
So there's a lot
less data there.

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:54.040
The invoice is really more about
here's how much I need to pay.

00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:56.710
All of these tables and
charts are much more about

00:06:56.710 --> 00:06:58.720
how those costs
actually came to be.

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:01.300
And the billing cost data is
really inside the billing cost

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:03.850
table is really
just a tabular view

00:07:03.850 --> 00:07:05.470
of all of your invoice costs.

00:07:05.470 --> 00:07:07.270
And it's grouped by the SKU.

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:09.520
You can quickly filter
by different projects.

00:07:09.520 --> 00:07:12.550
You can do services, SKUs,
you can rearrange the columns,

00:07:12.550 --> 00:07:16.270
it's a really great and simple
way to break down all the costs

00:07:16.270 --> 00:07:17.710
and see how they're grouped.

00:07:17.710 --> 00:07:20.290
You can also export it
to a CSV if you need

00:07:20.290 --> 00:07:22.360
to do some offline analysis.

00:07:22.360 --> 00:07:23.860
But this is probably
the first place

00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:28.060
where you'll be able to see
all those costs broken down.

00:07:28.060 --> 00:07:30.790
Similar to that table,
there's the pricing report.

00:07:30.790 --> 00:07:32.920
And the pricing report,
it looks very similar.

00:07:32.920 --> 00:07:35.087
But the difference here is
that this one is actually

00:07:35.087 --> 00:07:37.600
showing you the cost of
all the different services

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:38.980
that Google Cloud has.

00:07:38.980 --> 00:07:40.810
That actually
includes Google Cloud.

00:07:40.810 --> 00:07:43.505
And it includes Google
Maps Platform and G Suite.

00:07:43.505 --> 00:07:45.130
So they're all
available in that table.

00:07:45.130 --> 00:07:47.140
And similar to the
billing cost report,

00:07:47.140 --> 00:07:48.790
you can move things around.

00:07:48.790 --> 00:07:50.660
And you can break
it down as you need.

00:07:50.660 --> 00:07:53.470
But you're also able to select
to show all the SKUs for Google

00:07:53.470 --> 00:07:56.870
Cloud or just showing the
SKUs that you've used.

00:07:56.870 --> 00:07:59.920
So it's a quick way to get the
pricing for all of that data.

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:03.520
On top of, if you have a
negotiated price with Google

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:05.740
Cloud, it's going to
show you the default list

00:08:05.740 --> 00:08:08.650
price, the negotiated price
and the discount there.

00:08:08.650 --> 00:08:10.150
So it's really,
really helpful to be

00:08:10.150 --> 00:08:12.940
able to say, OK, well, these
are all the services cost.

00:08:12.940 --> 00:08:14.380
And just like that
billing report,

00:08:14.380 --> 00:08:16.930
you can export it to
a CSV so that you're

00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:20.260
able to see all that data and
do some offline analysis as

00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:22.180
needed.

00:08:22.180 --> 00:08:24.870
Now, the Billing documents page
is a fairly straightforward

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:25.500
page.

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:28.435
It's really just where you go to
download invoices, statements.

00:08:28.435 --> 00:08:30.060
It used to be called
the Invoices page.

00:08:30.060 --> 00:08:31.435
It's been renamed
and cleaned up.

00:08:31.435 --> 00:08:33.602
And, again, it's really
just about finding that data

00:08:33.602 --> 00:08:34.440
and downloading it.

00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:36.732
This is where you're going
to get the hard copy for all

00:08:36.732 --> 00:08:38.049
those documents.

00:08:38.049 --> 00:08:40.047
The billing reports,
the actual chart

00:08:40.047 --> 00:08:41.630
that you're seeing
on the screen here,

00:08:41.630 --> 00:08:43.100
this is part of the
Billing reports page.

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:44.475
And this is probably
where you're

00:08:44.475 --> 00:08:46.690
going to want to spend
most of your time, right?

00:08:46.690 --> 00:08:49.720
It gives you a great way to
visualize all of the data

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:51.070
that we were looking at before.

00:08:51.070 --> 00:08:54.070
The table is great because it
breaks down into great detail.

00:08:54.070 --> 00:08:56.440
But it can be a little
hard to digest, right?

00:08:56.440 --> 00:08:58.810
The visualization here
is really helpful.

00:08:58.810 --> 00:09:01.720
And it shows you the
transactional costs

00:09:01.720 --> 00:09:03.730
on a per day basis.

00:09:03.730 --> 00:09:06.370
And you can actually break it
between a line chart and a bar

00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:07.130
chart.

00:09:07.130 --> 00:09:09.530
But there's a lot of
options on this page.

00:09:09.530 --> 00:09:12.140
And that's where you'll be
measuring a lot of your costs.

00:09:12.140 --> 00:09:14.812
So one of the new features
here is the cost forecast.

00:09:14.812 --> 00:09:17.020
And this has been added and
changed a couple of times

00:09:17.020 --> 00:09:18.320
over the past year.

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:20.170
And it includes a
long-term cost trend.

00:09:20.170 --> 00:09:22.640
So now it includes
up to 12 months.

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:26.000
And it factors in things
like sustained use discounts.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:28.792
So sustained use
discounts typically

00:09:28.792 --> 00:09:30.250
start off with a
very low discount.

00:09:30.250 --> 00:09:31.930
And then as you go along,
the sustained use discount

00:09:31.930 --> 00:09:32.710
kicks in.

00:09:32.710 --> 00:09:34.478
And you get this
downwards pattern.

00:09:34.478 --> 00:09:35.770
And then the next month starts.

00:09:35.770 --> 00:09:36.980
And it goes back up.

00:09:36.980 --> 00:09:39.250
So the forecasting takes
all of that into effect

00:09:39.250 --> 00:09:43.000
and gives you a great idea of
where all that information is

00:09:43.000 --> 00:09:46.040
and what you'll be spending
over the next 12 months.

00:09:46.040 --> 00:09:48.607
In addition to that, the right
side of the billing reports,

00:09:48.607 --> 00:09:50.440
there's all these filters
on the right side.

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:52.960
And you can drill down into
any specific components

00:09:52.960 --> 00:09:54.105
that you want.

00:09:54.105 --> 00:09:55.480
When you change
these components,

00:09:55.480 --> 00:09:57.340
it will actually
add it to the URL.

00:09:57.340 --> 00:10:00.020
And then if you make a
custom report or something,

00:10:00.020 --> 00:10:02.000
you can just share
that URL with others.

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:04.450
So it's really easy to
drill down into an area

00:10:04.450 --> 00:10:06.665
and then share that with
the rest of your team.

00:10:06.665 --> 00:10:08.290
There's also been a
lot of new features

00:10:08.290 --> 00:10:10.000
that have been added
to the filters.

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:13.690
And one of the cool ones
is the invoice-centric view

00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:14.660
that you can click on.

00:10:14.660 --> 00:10:16.780
So in the default
view, you can select

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:17.920
whatever range you want--

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:20.527
last 30 days, 90 days,
or just custom ranges.

00:10:20.527 --> 00:10:21.610
And that's really helpful.

00:10:21.610 --> 00:10:23.920
But the invoice view
just tailors it directly

00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:25.150
to your invoice, right?

00:10:25.150 --> 00:10:26.680
So show me the
invoice for June--

00:10:26.680 --> 00:10:29.020
show me the invoice for
August or June and August.

00:10:29.020 --> 00:10:31.150
And, again, it breaks
down that chart

00:10:31.150 --> 00:10:33.950
so you can see the information
and the costs there.

00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:36.520
But it really just lets
you say, OK, well here's

00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:40.515
all the cost for that month and
how it compares to my invoice.

00:10:40.515 --> 00:10:41.890
In addition to
that filter, we've

00:10:41.890 --> 00:10:43.180
added a couple other ones.

00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:44.138
This one's really cool.

00:10:44.138 --> 00:10:45.430
It's location filter, right?

00:10:45.430 --> 00:10:47.620
So now you can actually
break down your costs

00:10:47.620 --> 00:10:50.110
by where they came
from, what geography,

00:10:50.110 --> 00:10:52.120
what region, or
multi-region area.

00:10:52.120 --> 00:10:53.680
All of those are
available here--

00:10:53.680 --> 00:10:56.290
just a really helpful way
to break down the costs.

00:10:56.290 --> 00:10:57.970
In addition to that,
we've got labels.

00:10:57.970 --> 00:10:59.812
We talked about
project labels before.

00:10:59.812 --> 00:11:01.270
And these are
resource labels where

00:11:01.270 --> 00:11:03.103
you're able to do the
same thing, all right.

00:11:03.103 --> 00:11:06.580
Let's take a compute engine
instance or multiple instances

00:11:06.580 --> 00:11:08.350
and label them all
with production.

00:11:08.350 --> 00:11:11.360
And now, in the billing cost
reports, you can actually say,

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:13.720
show me all those production
instances and the costs

00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:15.520
they generated--
really, really helpful

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:19.060
to be able to drill down
into that level of detail.

00:11:19.060 --> 00:11:22.710
So I mentioned this
before in the health card.

00:11:22.710 --> 00:11:24.643
But what you want to do--

00:11:24.643 --> 00:11:25.810
and here's a feature, right?

00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:28.560
You just go to the
Billing reports page.

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:30.690
You enable the
export to BigQuery.

00:11:30.690 --> 00:11:32.670
And what you want to
do is turn that on.

00:11:32.670 --> 00:11:35.670
If you haven't done it so
right now, pause, and then go

00:11:35.670 --> 00:11:38.110
do it because it's
really, really important.

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:40.750
It actually doesn't take data
from before you turn it on,

00:11:40.750 --> 00:11:42.750
which is why it's so
important that you probably

00:11:42.750 --> 00:11:44.470
want to go enable it now.

00:11:44.470 --> 00:11:48.802
But this exports all the billing
data at a very granular level.

00:11:48.802 --> 00:11:50.760
Some of the changes that
have happened recently

00:11:50.760 --> 00:11:52.150
is now going to it's hourly.

00:11:52.150 --> 00:11:54.660
So it has a start
hour and an end hour.

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:56.577
And then it's all the
costs that have happened

00:11:56.577 --> 00:11:59.077
for all of the different things
associated with that billing

00:11:59.077 --> 00:11:59.790
account.

00:11:59.790 --> 00:12:01.855
It is the most granular
data you're going to get.

00:12:01.855 --> 00:12:03.480
A BigQuery, of course,
is a great place

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:05.597
to do data analysis
and store data.

00:12:05.597 --> 00:12:07.680
So it's really, really
helpful to have that there.

00:12:07.680 --> 00:12:09.550
But you can always
export it out of BigQuery

00:12:09.550 --> 00:12:12.210
if you need to hook it up
to a third-party tool or BI

00:12:12.210 --> 00:12:13.353
or whatever.

00:12:13.353 --> 00:12:14.520
It's really helpful to have.

00:12:14.520 --> 00:12:16.170
And that's why it's so
important to make sure

00:12:16.170 --> 00:12:17.587
that you enable
it, because you're

00:12:17.587 --> 00:12:20.400
going to want to be able
to see that level of detail

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:22.260
when you do any
future cost modeling

00:12:22.260 --> 00:12:27.220
or really just to get that
level of analysis on your costs.

00:12:27.220 --> 00:12:30.150
So all of that really
focuses on the ways

00:12:30.150 --> 00:12:31.260
to understand your costs.

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:32.760
I want to talk about
the biggest way

00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:34.560
to control your costs, right?

00:12:34.560 --> 00:12:35.610
Viewing them is great.

00:12:35.610 --> 00:12:38.227
But being able to control
them is also very important.

00:12:38.227 --> 00:12:39.810
And we're going to
talk about budgets.

00:12:39.810 --> 00:12:41.602
So budgets are probably
the number one tool

00:12:41.602 --> 00:12:43.350
to actually implement
cost controls.

00:12:43.350 --> 00:12:44.592
And they're really flexible.

00:12:44.592 --> 00:12:46.050
They're actually
very powerful too.

00:12:46.050 --> 00:12:48.430
So let's go ahead
and break it down.

00:12:48.430 --> 00:12:50.590
Starting with budgets,
the first thing you can do

00:12:50.590 --> 00:12:52.773
is, well, you just
set up a budget.

00:12:52.773 --> 00:12:54.190
And when you set
up a budget, say,

00:12:54.190 --> 00:12:56.230
let's say $1,000
budget, when you

00:12:56.230 --> 00:12:59.110
hit that amount, it's
going to send an email out

00:12:59.110 --> 00:13:01.000
to billing administrators
saying, hey,

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:02.240
you hit this budget.

00:13:02.240 --> 00:13:04.390
But you can actually
configure multiple thresholds

00:13:04.390 --> 00:13:07.120
because you want to know
maybe before you hit $1,000.

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:10.180
So when you set up a budget,
you actually set a threshold.

00:13:10.180 --> 00:13:14.510
And you'll say 50% of $1,000
and then 100% of $1,000.

00:13:14.510 --> 00:13:17.300
But you can also set up
forecasted thresholds.

00:13:17.300 --> 00:13:19.270
So forecasted thresholds
are about when

00:13:19.270 --> 00:13:21.910
Google Cloud predicts that
you're going to hit that cost,

00:13:21.910 --> 00:13:22.510
right?

00:13:22.510 --> 00:13:24.093
And that can be
really helpful to stay

00:13:24.093 --> 00:13:25.300
on top of any surprises.

00:13:25.300 --> 00:13:27.145
So if you're three
days into the month,

00:13:27.145 --> 00:13:28.520
and you get an
email saying that,

00:13:28.520 --> 00:13:30.062
hey, Google Cloud
thinks you're going

00:13:30.062 --> 00:13:32.590
to spend 100% of your
$1,000 budget, that

00:13:32.590 --> 00:13:35.090
might be a call to action
to go do something.

00:13:35.090 --> 00:13:37.352
So as you set up a budget,
set up multiple thresholds.

00:13:37.352 --> 00:13:38.810
And you can set up
multiple budgets

00:13:38.810 --> 00:13:40.360
as well because
you're going to want

00:13:40.360 --> 00:13:42.250
to be able to track
different things.

00:13:42.250 --> 00:13:43.840
Speaking of that,
you can actually

00:13:43.840 --> 00:13:45.530
set up filters on the budget.

00:13:45.530 --> 00:13:49.250
So the budget by default is
the entire billing account.

00:13:49.250 --> 00:13:51.190
But when you actually
filter it down,

00:13:51.190 --> 00:13:54.160
you can choose specific
projects, specific products.

00:13:54.160 --> 00:13:56.000
Or you can even choose
those resource labels

00:13:56.000 --> 00:13:58.450
that we talked about earlier
and say, OK, well, show me.

00:13:58.450 --> 00:13:59.617
Here's my production budget.

00:13:59.617 --> 00:14:00.720
Here's my dev budget.

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:04.120
Here is the budget for
all of my biz dev project.

00:14:04.120 --> 00:14:06.580
You have that flexibility
and that control

00:14:06.580 --> 00:14:09.130
to be able to say, well, here,
we'll get different alerts

00:14:09.130 --> 00:14:10.220
for different people.

00:14:10.220 --> 00:14:12.560
The other thing I want to
talk about is the Budget API.

00:14:12.560 --> 00:14:13.930
So this is currently in beta.

00:14:13.930 --> 00:14:15.580
But what it lets
you do is automate

00:14:15.580 --> 00:14:18.200
the creation and managing
of those budgets.

00:14:18.200 --> 00:14:20.560
That's really important
because, as you grow

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:22.840
and your organization gets
more and more complex,

00:14:22.840 --> 00:14:25.330
you probably don't want
to do all of this by hand.

00:14:25.330 --> 00:14:27.500
So being able to
spend some time,

00:14:27.500 --> 00:14:29.770
learn the Budget API
is really, really

00:14:29.770 --> 00:14:32.170
critical to being able to
say, OK, well, I set up

00:14:32.170 --> 00:14:32.980
a new project.

00:14:32.980 --> 00:14:35.022
We're going to go ahead
and automate and add that

00:14:35.022 --> 00:14:37.070
to budgets and create
new budgets and so on.

00:14:37.070 --> 00:14:40.902
So spending some time here can
be really valuable as you grow.

00:14:40.902 --> 00:14:43.360
So we've talked about budgets
being able to send out emails

00:14:43.360 --> 00:14:44.740
to billing administrators.

00:14:44.740 --> 00:14:47.865
But oftentimes, you want to send
emails to other people as well.

00:14:47.865 --> 00:14:49.240
And that's where
this new feature

00:14:49.240 --> 00:14:51.280
comes in that can
actually let you add up

00:14:51.280 --> 00:14:54.682
to five emails per budget
through cloud monitoring.

00:14:54.682 --> 00:14:56.390
And it's actually
pretty straightforward.

00:14:56.390 --> 00:14:57.640
You just go into the budget.

00:14:57.640 --> 00:15:00.040
You click on the little checkbox
that says cloud monitoring.

00:15:00.040 --> 00:15:01.415
You're going to
set up a channel.

00:15:01.415 --> 00:15:03.100
And then you just
add emails to that.

00:15:03.100 --> 00:15:06.100
An important note here is
that while you can just

00:15:06.100 --> 00:15:08.050
add five individual
emails, you might

00:15:08.050 --> 00:15:10.690
want to consider adding a
group email instead, right?

00:15:10.690 --> 00:15:13.570
So that way instead of sending
it to one person in accounting,

00:15:13.570 --> 00:15:16.360
you're sending it to a group in
accounting or to a biz dev team

00:15:16.360 --> 00:15:19.510
or whatever, because you
want to be able to say, here,

00:15:19.510 --> 00:15:21.640
send these budget alert
emails to this team.

00:15:21.640 --> 00:15:23.860
But you don't want to have
to constantly add people

00:15:23.860 --> 00:15:25.510
to the team to
the budget, right?

00:15:25.510 --> 00:15:27.635
So it's much easier to
manage the team in something

00:15:27.635 --> 00:15:30.800
like G Suite and then just have
that send out to that group.

00:15:30.800 --> 00:15:32.990
And then you can leave
the budgets alone.

00:15:32.990 --> 00:15:35.080
So it's a great way to
add additional people

00:15:35.080 --> 00:15:36.850
to your budget.

00:15:36.850 --> 00:15:38.920
Now, that's the
basics of budgets.

00:15:38.920 --> 00:15:40.880
But the real power
of budgets come

00:15:40.880 --> 00:15:44.327
from what we call programmatic
budget notifications.

00:15:44.327 --> 00:15:46.160
And this is really cool
because it basically

00:15:46.160 --> 00:15:49.040
lets you hook up your
budget to Pub/Sub.

00:15:49.040 --> 00:15:51.420
And Pub/Sub in this case
is an event handler.

00:15:51.420 --> 00:15:53.433
And it's going to take
alerts from the budget.

00:15:53.433 --> 00:15:55.850
And they'll send them off to
wherever you want them to be.

00:15:55.850 --> 00:15:58.580
And we've got some sample
code in the documentation

00:15:58.580 --> 00:16:01.850
that actually looks at
sending it to Cloud Functions.

00:16:01.850 --> 00:16:03.830
But when you create
a budget, you just

00:16:03.830 --> 00:16:05.330
click that box-- very simple.

00:16:05.330 --> 00:16:07.580
You choose a Pub/Sub topic,
or you make a new one.

00:16:07.580 --> 00:16:10.160
And then you can go off and
make your Cloud Function.

00:16:10.160 --> 00:16:12.840
And we've got some
sample code like I said.

00:16:12.840 --> 00:16:15.680
A very popular example of
this is sending a budget

00:16:15.680 --> 00:16:17.450
notification to Slack, right?

00:16:17.450 --> 00:16:19.490
So whenever you're on
an update on your budget

00:16:19.490 --> 00:16:21.470
comes in, go put it
in a Slack channel

00:16:21.470 --> 00:16:23.120
and have an update there.

00:16:23.120 --> 00:16:25.320
Simple, but it's
just a notification.

00:16:25.320 --> 00:16:27.770
So when you have Google Cloud
Functions and other systems

00:16:27.770 --> 00:16:29.630
in Google Cloud,
you can do whatever

00:16:29.630 --> 00:16:30.890
you could do with code.

00:16:30.890 --> 00:16:33.057
And that's really important
because you can actually

00:16:33.057 --> 00:16:36.230
do more advanced
things, like, let's say

00:16:36.230 --> 00:16:38.817
you hit way higher on your
budget than you expected.

00:16:38.817 --> 00:16:40.400
You could actually
shut down projects.

00:16:40.400 --> 00:16:41.997
You could shut down resources.

00:16:41.997 --> 00:16:44.330
There's some sample code for
detaching a billing account

00:16:44.330 --> 00:16:45.320
from a project.

00:16:45.320 --> 00:16:47.038
That's a really
dangerous thing to do,

00:16:47.038 --> 00:16:48.830
because when you remove
the billing account

00:16:48.830 --> 00:16:51.620
for the project, any paid
resource on that project

00:16:51.620 --> 00:16:54.110
might get shut down in
a very unexpected way.

00:16:54.110 --> 00:16:55.860
So be really careful about that.

00:16:55.860 --> 00:16:58.670
But it's a great starting point
to understand how do I really

00:16:58.670 --> 00:17:00.390
control my resources?

00:17:00.390 --> 00:17:02.540
And, again, it comes down
to your organization.

00:17:02.540 --> 00:17:04.980
What's important for
your organization?

00:17:04.980 --> 00:17:07.214
How do those budgets come
up for your organization?

00:17:07.214 --> 00:17:08.839
And then what kind
of responses can you

00:17:08.839 --> 00:17:11.660
have to really putting
controls in place?

00:17:11.660 --> 00:17:13.460
So programmatic
budget notifications

00:17:13.460 --> 00:17:15.740
are really going to be
the most powerful way

00:17:15.740 --> 00:17:18.400
to put cost controls
in your place.

00:17:18.400 --> 00:17:21.400
So talked about understanding,
talked about control.

00:17:21.400 --> 00:17:23.131
Let's talk about
optimizing cost, right?

00:17:23.131 --> 00:17:25.089
This is the great one
because basically, you're

00:17:25.089 --> 00:17:26.680
going to end up saving money.

00:17:26.680 --> 00:17:30.380
And hopefully you're going to
do it without changing anything.

00:17:30.380 --> 00:17:32.440
And honestly, one of the
more common things I see

00:17:32.440 --> 00:17:34.780
is that people aren't taking
advantage of committed use

00:17:34.780 --> 00:17:35.800
discounts.

00:17:35.800 --> 00:17:38.290
And this is such a huge
and actually really easy

00:17:38.290 --> 00:17:40.630
thing to set up on your account.

00:17:40.630 --> 00:17:42.930
All you have to do
is sign up for them.

00:17:42.930 --> 00:17:45.430
And you'll start to pay a lot
less for some of the resources

00:17:45.430 --> 00:17:46.473
you already use.

00:17:46.473 --> 00:17:48.640
So committed use discounts
or sometimes we call them

00:17:48.640 --> 00:17:50.770
CUDs are able to help
you save on really

00:17:50.770 --> 00:17:52.580
great static workloads.

00:17:52.580 --> 00:17:57.370
So if you've got VCPUs, RAM,
SSDs, GPUs, all of those

00:17:57.370 --> 00:18:00.350
at a static level, you can
look at and say, OK, well,

00:18:00.350 --> 00:18:01.930
I'm running a good
amount of that.

00:18:01.930 --> 00:18:03.810
Or maybe I have a
baseline of it, right?

00:18:03.810 --> 00:18:05.440
You might have spiky workloads.

00:18:05.440 --> 00:18:07.270
But if you've got
that baseline, you

00:18:07.270 --> 00:18:09.610
can take them out and purchase
a committed use discount

00:18:09.610 --> 00:18:10.630
for that amount.

00:18:10.630 --> 00:18:13.180
And then you're just going
to be paying a lot less

00:18:13.180 --> 00:18:14.590
for the same thing.

00:18:14.590 --> 00:18:16.382
You're not changing
those resources at all.

00:18:16.382 --> 00:18:17.965
You're just getting
a discounted price

00:18:17.965 --> 00:18:20.560
because you're signing up for
a one or three-year commitment

00:18:20.560 --> 00:18:21.558
based on that.

00:18:21.558 --> 00:18:22.850
So it's really helpful to have.

00:18:22.850 --> 00:18:24.450
It's really easy to set up.

00:18:24.450 --> 00:18:27.090
And I've actually just added
[? Invada, ?] the committed use

00:18:27.090 --> 00:18:28.870
discounts for Cloud SQL.

00:18:28.870 --> 00:18:30.483
The database is
probably not something

00:18:30.483 --> 00:18:31.900
you're spinning
up and down a lot.

00:18:31.900 --> 00:18:33.730
You probably have a
good baseline for that.

00:18:33.730 --> 00:18:36.437
So it's just another great
way to save a lot of money.

00:18:36.437 --> 00:18:38.770
And we actually have the
Committed Use Discount Analysis

00:18:38.770 --> 00:18:41.920
Report now, again, available
in the Billing section, which

00:18:41.920 --> 00:18:44.230
lets you just visualize
your usage, right?

00:18:44.230 --> 00:18:46.660
So in this case, you
have a very static usage.

00:18:46.660 --> 00:18:48.920
It'll show you what you're
using on a committed use

00:18:48.920 --> 00:18:51.610
discount, what you
could be using,

00:18:51.610 --> 00:18:53.080
and what's eligible for it.

00:18:53.080 --> 00:18:54.560
And then you can
say, well, look,

00:18:54.560 --> 00:18:56.170
I've got this flat
workload that's

00:18:56.170 --> 00:18:57.760
going to be running for a year.

00:18:57.760 --> 00:18:59.260
Why don't I save money on?

00:18:59.260 --> 00:19:01.540
Very easy way to save money.

00:19:01.540 --> 00:19:03.340
And, again, no changes
really necessary

00:19:03.340 --> 00:19:06.810
to your actual resources
that are running.

00:19:06.810 --> 00:19:09.260
So the other thing that
changed in the last year

00:19:09.260 --> 00:19:11.630
with committed use discounts
is discount sharing.

00:19:11.630 --> 00:19:13.130
And this one is
probably my favorite

00:19:13.130 --> 00:19:15.950
because what it lets you do is
take committed use discounts

00:19:15.950 --> 00:19:18.523
and move them to a
billing account level.

00:19:18.523 --> 00:19:19.940
If you've worked
with them before,

00:19:19.940 --> 00:19:21.740
they were specific to a project.

00:19:21.740 --> 00:19:23.320
So you have committed
use discount

00:19:23.320 --> 00:19:25.520
on this project and one
on this and this and this.

00:19:25.520 --> 00:19:28.170
And as you grow on projects,
that can be pretty painful.

00:19:28.170 --> 00:19:31.025
So it's really great to be
able to say, all the billing,

00:19:31.025 --> 00:19:32.650
all the committed
use discounts are now

00:19:32.650 --> 00:19:34.430
at the billing account level--

00:19:34.430 --> 00:19:36.990
much, much easier
and easier to say,

00:19:36.990 --> 00:19:39.170
oh, let's just spread it
across all our projects.

00:19:39.170 --> 00:19:40.820
Highly recommend
you check that out

00:19:40.820 --> 00:19:43.195
if you're using committed use
discounts because it's just

00:19:43.195 --> 00:19:45.882
an even easier
way to save money.

00:19:45.882 --> 00:19:47.840
One of the other features
that's rolled out now

00:19:47.840 --> 00:19:49.923
and is going to have a lot
of changes coming to it

00:19:49.923 --> 00:19:51.500
soon is the
Recommender Hub which

00:19:51.500 --> 00:19:53.210
is part of an Active Assist.

00:19:53.210 --> 00:19:54.200
It's currently in beta.

00:19:54.200 --> 00:19:56.777
And it surfaces a lot
of recommendations.

00:19:56.777 --> 00:19:58.610
So you'll see some
security recommendations.

00:19:58.610 --> 00:20:01.160
But you'll also see some
cost recommendations.

00:20:01.160 --> 00:20:03.620
And two of the great ones
are a committed use discount

00:20:03.620 --> 00:20:04.970
recommendation.

00:20:04.970 --> 00:20:06.678
So the analysis report
lets you see it.

00:20:06.678 --> 00:20:08.720
But this one will just
tell you, look, hey, look,

00:20:08.720 --> 00:20:10.310
you're spending a lot here.

00:20:10.310 --> 00:20:11.900
You could be saving money there.

00:20:11.900 --> 00:20:14.000
And then another
[INAUDIBLE] recommendation

00:20:14.000 --> 00:20:17.330
is the Compute Engine cross
family recommendations.

00:20:17.330 --> 00:20:18.900
So when you're using
Compute Engine,

00:20:18.900 --> 00:20:20.900
you've got to analyze
which family you're using,

00:20:20.900 --> 00:20:22.220
which sizes you're using.

00:20:22.220 --> 00:20:23.900
That can get pretty complicated.

00:20:23.900 --> 00:20:25.940
And this will basically
measure the amount

00:20:25.940 --> 00:20:27.898
that you're using those
and say, oh, look, this

00:20:27.898 --> 00:20:30.500
is a pretty memory-intensive
application you're running.

00:20:30.500 --> 00:20:32.000
You might want to
consider switching

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:33.630
to a memory-optimized machine.

00:20:33.630 --> 00:20:35.362
And you'll end up
saving a lot of money.

00:20:35.362 --> 00:20:37.820
There's a lot of great stuff
coming to the Recommender Hub,

00:20:37.820 --> 00:20:40.620
so make sure to keep
your eye on that.

00:20:40.620 --> 00:20:42.320
So with all of
that being said, we

00:20:42.320 --> 00:20:44.930
talked about understanding,
controlling, and really

00:20:44.930 --> 00:20:46.400
optimizing your cost.

00:20:46.400 --> 00:20:49.310
There's two great sessions that
are also available this week--

00:20:49.310 --> 00:20:51.620
CST 101 and CST 104.

00:20:51.620 --> 00:20:53.240
You can see a great
customer story

00:20:53.240 --> 00:20:55.910
of how they used these
recommendations to save money

00:20:55.910 --> 00:20:57.440
on a whole bunch
of other stuff--

00:20:57.440 --> 00:20:59.400
definitely recommend
you check that out.

00:20:59.400 --> 00:21:01.680
And if you're interested
in learning more,

00:21:01.680 --> 00:21:04.055
you want it set up to this
